The genealogy of Jesus' family.
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. And Isaac was the father of Jacob. And Jacob was the father of John and his brothers.
Judah the father of Perez and Zerah, and Tamar their mother. Perez the father of Hezron. And Hezron the father of Aram.
He was the father of Amminadab. He was the father of Nahshon. He was the father of Solomon.
The salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of David the king. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon is the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam is the father of Abijah. Abijah is the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat, and Jehoshaphat the father of Joram, and Joram the father of Ahaz.
Azia was the father of Jotham, Jotham was the father of Ahaz, and Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Menahem, Menahem was the father of Amon, Amon was the father of Jehoiakim.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiakim and his brothers. And in that time, the Jewish people were forced to migrate to Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azir.
Azur was a father to Zadok, and Zadok was a father to Akim, and Akim was a father to Eliud.
Eliakim was the father of Elishama. Elishama was the father of Pethuel. Pethuel was the father of Guni.
Jacob was the father of John. John was the husband of Mary, who gave birth to Jesus, called the Christ.
There were 14 generations from Abraham to David. There were 14 generations from David until the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon. There were 14 generations from that time until Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother Mary was promised in marriage to Joseph. But before they came together, it became clear that she would give birth to a child. She conceived through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusuf, was a pious man. He did not wish to embarrass her publicly. Therefore, he planned to divorce her quietly.
But while Joseph was pondering this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary as your wife. For the child who is conceived in her is of the Holy Spirit."
You will have a son, and you must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet: "The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and they will call him Immanuel."
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and he will be called Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God with us."
Joseph awoke, and did what the angel of the Lord commanded him to do. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not embrace her until she bore a son. And Joseph gave the child the name Jesus.
The Magi visited Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea during the time when Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose, and we have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and everyone in Jerusalem was also disturbed.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the people's leaders, including the assembled crowds. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written through the prophet:
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come for me one who will be ruler over Israel, and whose coming forth is from of old, from ancient days." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. He learned from them the exact time when the star appeared.
He sent them to Bethlehem and said, "Go and look carefully for the young boy. And when you find him, bring me word so that I may also go and worship him."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they continued on their way. And the star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. And when they came to Jerusalem, they asked for
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another way. The flight of Jesus' family to Egypt
After the wise men had gone, Joseph had a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to start looking for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
And they remained there until King Herod died. And thus the words of the Lord, which He spoke through the prophet, were fulfilled. For He had said, "I have surely made mention of you and have kept you in My heart." (Jeremiah 31:3)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him, and he became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the male children who were under the age of two were to be killed. This corresponded to the time when the wise men had seen the star.
And in this way the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he said:
A voice is heard in Ramah, a voice of weeping and great mourning. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth
After Herod's death, Joseph had a dream while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who tried to kill the child are dead."
Then Joseph arose and took the Child and His mother Mary and returned with them to the land of Israel.
But afterward he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. So Joseph became afraid to go there. Because of a warning he saw in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee.
And he lived there in a city called Nazareth. And thus the prophecies of the prophets about Jesus were fulfilled. For they said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Disobey what you are doing of sins! For the dominion of the heavens is near."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke, saying: "A voice of one calling in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people came to him from Jerusalem and all Judea. And they came also from all the regions around the Jordan River.
When they confessed their errors, John baptized them in the River Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees approaching the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
Produce fruits that show you have separated from your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe is already at the roots of the trees. Every tree that does not produce good fruit will be cut down. It will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, I call upon you to repent for your sins. But after me will come one who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
Here is the translation of the provided text: "His sickle is in his hand for clearing the straw from his harvested field. He will gather his wheat in the storage room. But the straw will burn with unquenchable fire." Jesus is baptized.
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan River. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to stop him. "I need *you* to baptize me," John said. "Why would you ever come to me for this?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now. It is right for us to do this. He is carrying out God's holy plan." John agreed.
At the moment Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. And at that moment, the heavens were opened. And Jesus saw the Spirit of God descend upon him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love. I am very pleased with him."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. And there, the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus felt hungry.
The tempter came to him, saying, "If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.' " (Exodus 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He put him on the highest point of the Temple.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11, 12)
Jesus answered, saying, "It is written again, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.' " (Exodus 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you bow down to me and worship me," he said, "I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written, 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him only you shall serve.' " (Exodus 13:6)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to preach.
John the Baptist was put in prison. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and moved to live in the city of Capernaum. It was located on the shore of the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
And in this way the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah was fulfilled. For he said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee where non-Jews live! Land on the Mediterranean coast! Lands east of the Jordan River!
Those who now live in darkness will see a great light. For they are living in a very dark land; but a light will shine on them. (Isaiah 9:1,2)
Since that time, Jesus began to preach. "Embrace your repentance," he said. "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets immediately and followed him.
From there, he saw two other brothers. They were James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. While they were preparing their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus traveled throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of the kingdom of God. He healed every disease and every weakness the people had.
His news spread throughout Syria. People brought him everyone suffering from various diseases. Some were suffering from severe pain. Others were possessed by demons. Some were trembling violently. Others could not move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
Followed by a great multitude. Some people came from Galilee and from a region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. And others came from the region beyond the Jordan River.
Jesus bestows blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds, so he went up a mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
The blessed are those who are spiritually needy. The kingdom of heaven belongs to them.
The meek are those who mourn, and they will be comforted.
The blessed are those whose heart is empty of pride. The land will be given to them.
Blessed are the hungry and those who desire justice. They will be satisfied.
The righteous people are those who show mercy, and God will show mercy to them.
The blessed are those whose hearts are pure. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are they which are persecuted for righteousness' sake: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when men revile you, and persecute you, and say all manner of evil against you falsely, for my sake. Blessed are you, when men shall revile you, and persecute you, and shall say all manner of evil against you, some true, some false; for my sake.
Be happy and joyful. Your reward is great in heaven. In the same way, people hurt the prophets who lived a long time ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But what if the salt loses its saltiness? How can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. It will be trampled underfoot by people.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket. Instead, they put it on a stand, where it gives light to everyone in the house.
No one lights a lamp and puts it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on a lampstand. Then the light that comes from it illuminates everyone in the house.
Similarly, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the law its complete meaning.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish, but to fulfill, and to give the full meaning of the Law.
What I am about to tell you is true. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not an smallest stroke of a pen in the law will disappear until everything is accomplished.
Do not even break the least of the commandments. And do not teach others to break them. If you do, you will be called the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven. Instead, practice and teach these commandments. Then you will be called an important person in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Here is what I say to you: You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. To murder ...
I have heard what was said to people long ago. It was said to them, "You shall not murder" (Exodus 20:13). Everyone who murders will be held accountable for their action.
But here is what I tell you: Do not be angry with your brother. For whoever is angry with his brother will be brought before the court. And whoever says to his brother, 'You good-for-nothing!' will be brought before the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, 'You fool!' will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Imagine that you are offering your gift at the altar. And you remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled to your brother, and then come and offer your gift.
Imagine that someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do it while you are still with him on your way. If you don't, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am about to tell you is real. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery" (Exodus 20:14).
But here is what I tell you: do not look at a woman in a wrong way. Whoever does that has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to stumble, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter life maimed than with two eyes to be thrown into hellfire. Your eye is not a part of your body, it is merely an extension of your being. To remove it is to sacrifice a part of yourself, but it is better to be maimed than to be consumed by hellfire.
"If your right hand is causing you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. For your hand is just a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell." Divorce.
It was said, "If a man divorces his wife, he must give her a certificate of divorce." (Leviticus 24:1)
But here is what I am telling you: Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man does not have the right to divorce his wife unless she has been unfaithful to him. The Right Wing
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the covenant you have made with the Lord your God. You must keep the commands he has given you and the covenant he has made with you. You must not go after other gods, those gods the people in the land you are entering worship. For the Lord your God is a consuming fire, a jealous God. He will not let anyone despise him. He will not let anyone disregard his covenant, but will keep the covenant he has made forever."
But here's what I'm telling you. Never make promises like that. Don't make them in the name of heaven. It's God's throne.
Do not make them in the name of the earth; it is the footstool of God. Do not make them in the name of Jerusalem; it is the city of the Great King.
Do not swear by your head. You cannot make a single hair white or black.
Let "yes" mean "yes." And let "no" mean "no." Anything else comes from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here is what I tell you: Do not resist an evil person. "If someone strikes you on your right cheek, offer him your left cheek as well.
Suppose someone brings you to court for your shirt. Let him have your coat as well.
Imagine someone is forcing you to walk one mile. Walk two miles with him.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not refuse to lend something to someone who wants to borrow it. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, "Love your neighbor" (Leviticus 19:18) and hate your enemy.
But here's what I'm telling you: Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
"Then you will be the children of your Father in heaven. 'He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collectors do that.
If you only greet your own people, what more are you doing than others? Even unbelievers do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving to those in need.
Beware not to do "good deeds" in front of others. Do not do them to be seen by people. If you do, the Mute in the sky will not reward you.
When you give to the poor, do not announce it with trumpets. Do not be like the hypocrites, who love to pray standing in the temples and on the street corners, so that others may see them. I tell you the truth, they have received their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then you are given a secret gift. Your father will reward you. He sees what you are secretly doing. Prayer
When you pray, don't be like those who pretend to be pious. They love to stand and pray in temples and on street corners. They want others to see them. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have already received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room. Close the door and pray to your Father who cannot be seen. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret.
When you pray, do not go on babbling like irreligious people do. They think they will be heard because of their many words.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
Thus you should pray: "Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
He kept us from falling into sin when we are tempted. He saved us from Satan.
Forgive people when they wrong you. If you do, your Father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
When you go without eating, don't look gloomy like those who are called saints only. They express their extreme sadness. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I will tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
But when you go without eating, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then others will not know that you are fasting. Only your unseen Father will know. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret. Keep your treasures in heaven
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up your treasures in heaven, where neither moths nor rust consume, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eyes are good, your whole body will be filled with light."
But what if your eyes are bad? Your whole body will be in darkness. If the light within you is darkness, it means the darkness is extremely intense!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and money. Don't worry.
I tell you, do not worry. Do not worry about your life, what you eat or drink. And do not worry about your body and what you wear. Is there nothing more than eating in life? Are there not things more important for the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the sky, they do not sow nor do they gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not worth more than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you worry about clothing? Look at how the wild flowers grow. They do not spin, nor do they weave clothing.
But here is what I say to you: even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these.
If this is how God decorates wild grass, will He not decorate you even better? After all, grass is only here today. Tomorrow it will be thrown into the fire. Your faith is too small!"
Therefore do not worry. Do not say, "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People who are not God's followers pant after all those things. Your Father in heaven knows you need them.
But make the Kingdom of God your priority. Do what He wants you to do. And then you will be given all those other things as well.
Do not worry about what is to come, for tomorrow will take care of itself. Each day has enough struggle of its own.
Be fair when you judge others.
Do not judge others. Thus, no one will judge you.
You will be judged by the same standard you judge others. And you will be measured by the same measure you measure others.
You look at the piece of wood in your friend's eye, but you do not notice the piece of wood in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend, "Let me remove the piece of wood from your eye"? How can you say that when there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
O you who claim to know! First, take the piece of wood out of your own eye. Then you will be able to see well enough to remove the splinter of wood from your friend's eye.
Do not cast your pearls before swine. Let your speech be always with grace, seasoned with salt, that you may know how you ought to answer each one.
Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and the door will be opened to you.
He who asks receives, he who seeks finds, and he who knocks has the door opened for him.
What if your son asked for bread? Some of you would give him a stone?
Or imagine that he asked for a fish. Which one of you would give him a snake?
Even though you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your heavenly Father give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what was written in the Torah and the Prophets. The great and small doors.
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is wide that leads to destruction and hell. Many people walk down this road.
But the door is narrow and the way is rough that leads to life. Only a few people find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You will know them by their fruits. "Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles?
Likewise, every good tree produces good fruit, but a bad tree produces bad fruit.
No good tree can produce bad fruit, nor can a bad tree produce good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
You can distinguish every tree by its fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only those who do the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day, "Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not do many miracles in your name?"
Then I will clearly say to them, "I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!" The wise builder and the foolish builder.
Whoever hears my words and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The rains come. The water rises. The winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them is like a foolish man. He builds his house on the sand.
Rain falls. The water rises. The wind blows and hits that house. And it falls with a loud noise.
Jesus finished saying all these things. And the crowd was moved by his teachings.
He taught as one who had authority, and did not teach like their teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain. A great crowd followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus, saying, "Lord, if you are willing to make me clean, you can do it."
Jesus reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing to do so," he said. "Be clean!" Immediately the man was healed from his skin disease.
Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone. Go and show yourself to the priest. Offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman leader believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
He said, "My lord, my servant is lying down in the house and cannot move. He is suffering terribly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Sir, I am not worthy for you to enter my house. But if you just say the word, my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to one, "Go," and he goes. And I say to another, "Come," and he comes. And I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. He said to those who were following him, "What I am about to tell you is true. I have not found anyone in Israel with such great faith.
I tell you, many will come from the east and the west, and they will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will be seated with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But as for those who think that they are among the citizens of the kingdom, they will be cast out into the outer darkness. And there they will be howling and gnashing their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman commander, "Go, and it will be as you believe." And at that hour his servant was healed. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying down in bed. She was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and began to wait on Him.
When evening came, they brought to Jesus many who were possessed by demons. And he cast out the spirits with a word, and he healed all who were sick.
He did this to confirm what the prophet Isaiah had said, who had said: "He bore the suffering that we should have borne. And He bore the diseases that should have been ours" (Isaiah 53:4). The Cost of Following Jesus
Jesus saw the crowds around him, so he instructed his disciples to cross to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of the law came and said to him, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered and said, "The foxes have holes, and the birds of the air have nests; but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay His head."
One of his disciples said to him, "Master, let me go first to bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me, and let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms a Storm
Jesus climbed into the boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly a terrible storm blew up on the lake. The waves swept over the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples went and woke him up, saying, "Lord, save us! We are perishing!"
