The disciples were amazed and asked, "Who is this man? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men possessed by demons.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, in the region of the two walled cities. Two men who were possessed by demons approached him. They had come from the tombs. The men were so violent that no one could travel that way.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you not come here to punish us before the time of our reckoning?"
It was not very far away, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
And he was given power over the unclean spirits, to cast them out and to heal every disease and every affliction. And unclean spirits came out, crying out, "You have come to destroy us!" And Jesus rebuked them, and the unclean spirits came out and entered the pigs. And the herd of pigs, about two thousand, rushed down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.
Jesus said to them, "Go." So the evil spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd rushed down the steep hillside. They entered the lake and drowned in the water.
Those who had been tending the pigs had fled. They went to the city and told all this. They told the people what had happened to the men on whom the impure spirits had come.
Then the whole city went out to welcome Jesus. When they saw him, they pleaded with him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus got into the boat. He went to the other side of the lake and arrived at his own town.
Some men brought to him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. And Jesus saw their faith, and he said to the man, "Be of good cheer, my son; your sins are forgiven you."
Then some of the scholars of the law said to themselves, "This man says something that is very wicked!"
Jesus knew what was in their hearts. And he said, "Why do you have evil thoughts in your hearts?
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven" or to say, "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then He spoke to the man who could not walk. "Rise up," He said to him. "Take up your bed and go to your house."
The man got up and went home.
When the crowd saw it, they were filled with awe. They praised God for giving such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
And as Jesus was leaving from there, he saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax office. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Matthew rose up and followed Jesus.
Later, Jesus had dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and sinners came. They ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this, and said, "Those who are well do not need a physician, but those who are ill.
Go and learn what "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6) means. I did not come to follow those who believe they are right with God, but I came to follow sinners. "Jesus asks about fasting"
One day, the disciples of John came. And they said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast, but why do not your disciples fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. The new piece will wear away from the old one. That will make the holes worse.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins; if they do, the wineskins burst, the wine spills out, and the wineskins are ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins. And this preserves both the wineskins and the wine. (A dead girl and a suffering woman)
While Jesus was saying this, the chief of a priest came. He fell on his knees before Jesus, and said, "My daughter has just died. But come and put your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus rose up and walked with him. And his disciples did likewise.
At that moment, a woman approached Jesus from behind. She had been suffering from a disease that caused her to bleed, and it had been with her for twelve years. She touched the edge of his robe.
"I thought, 'All I need to do is touch his clothes. Then I will be cured.'"
When Jesus was leaving and saw a woman standing there, He said to her, "Woman, do not give up on hope." And she said to Him, "Sir, I don't have any faith until someone comes and carries me." And at that moment, her eyes were opened and she was healed.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the audience was removed, Jesus entered. He took the girl by the hand, and she arose.
News spread throughout the region about what Jesus had done. Jesus heals two blind men.
And Jesus, going out from there, followed by two blind men, who, crying out, said, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus entered the house, the blind came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" They answered, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes. And he said, "It shall be to you as you have believed."
They were able to see once again. Jesus strongly warned them, saying, "Make sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it everywhere in that area.
And as they were coming out, another man was brought to Jesus. He was controlled by a demon, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The crowd was astonished. They said, "Such a thing has never been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are only a few workers
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages. He taught in their synagogues, and preached the gospel of the kingdom. He healed all sickness and all weakness.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a deep concern for them. They were defeated and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few.
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field.
Jesus Sends Out the Twelve Disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to him, and he gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and affliction.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. First, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
After that were Philip and Bartholomew, and Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. Two others were James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who later betrayed Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent the twelve out, saying to them, "Do not go among the Gentiles or enter any town of the Samaritans.
Instead, go to the people of Israel. They are like stray sheep.
As you go, preach this message: 'The Kingdom of Heaven is near.'
Healing the sick. Raising the dead to life. The lepers are restored to purity. The demons are cast out. You received freely, so give freely.
You shall not carry with you gold or silver or copper in your purses.
Do not take a travel bag. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker must be provided with what they need.
When you enter a town or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at that person's home until you leave.
When you enter the house, greet those who live there.
If that house welcomes you, then bless it with peace. Otherwise, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. If they do not, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am about to tell you is real. On the day of reckoning it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Beware! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their churches.
You will be presented as rulers and kings because of me. You will be witnesses against them and against the non-Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words will be given to you to say.
It will not be you who speaks, but the spirit of your father will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver their brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their children. Children will rise up against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But the one who endures to the end will be saved.
If people attack you in a certain place, flee to another place. What I am going to tell you is the truth. You will not complete the journey in the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is no better than his teacher. And the servant is no better than his master.
It is enough for the student to be like his teacher. And it is enough for the servant to be like his master. If the head of the household is called Beelzebub, what are the others who live there going to be called?
Do not fear your enemies. What is hidden will be revealed. What is buried will be discovered.
What I tell you in the dark, speak in the light. What is whispered in your ear, shout from the rooftops.
Fear not those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul. Rather, fear the one who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Is it not sold, two birds for a farthing, for a single coin? Yet not one of them falls to the ground except in the knowledge of his father.
He even counts every single hair on your head!
Do not be afraid. You are more valuable than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make "sons against their fathers. Daughters will refuse to obey their mothers. Sons' mothers-in-law will stand against their own mothers-in-law.
The man's enemies will be those of his own household. (Micah 7:6)
He who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. And he who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And everyone who does not take up their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
He who finds his life will lose it, and he who loses his life for my sake will find it.
He who welcomes you welcomes me, and he who welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
If someone receives a prophet as a prophet, they will receive the reward of a prophet. And if someone receives a righteous person as a righteous person, they will receive the reward of a righteous person.
If someone gives a glass of cold water to a little child following me, what happens? What I am going to tell you now is true. He will certainly be rewarded.
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus completed the teaching of his twelve disciples. He then moved on to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ had done, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one we are waiting for? Or should we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go to John. Tell him what you hear and what you see.
The blind are given sight. The lame walk. Those with skin diseases are cured. The deaf hear. The dead are raised. Good news is proclaimed to the poor.
"The blessed are those who do not abandon their faith because of me."
And as John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John. And he said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
If it is not that, then what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. People who dress in fine clothes are in the palaces of kings.
So what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, he is more than a prophet.
He is the one mentioned in the Holy Book. It says: "I will send my messenger to prepare the way before me." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am about to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born more important than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is more important than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing. And those violent men seize it by force.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Let he who has ears hear.
Who can I compare the people of this generation to? They are like children sitting in the city markets, calling out to others. They say:
We played the flute for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song. But you did not become sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and 'sinners.' "But wisdom is justified by her deeds." The towns that did not repent were subjected to a more severe punishment.
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had done most of His miracles. His adversaries did not repent of their sins. And He said:
How dreadful it will be for you, Chorazin! How dreadful it will be for you, Bethsaida! If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented in sackcloth and ashes long ago. They would have sat in their ashes.
But I tell you this: in the day of judgment, Tyre and Sidon will be in a better condition than you are.
And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No, but you will be brought down to Hades. Imagine if the miracles that happened in you had happened in Sodom, it would be sitting there today.
But I tell you this: on the day of judgment, Sodom will have an easier time than you will." Rest for all who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "I glorify you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned and revealed them to little children. Yes, Father, because this is what you were pleased to do."
Yes, Dad, that's what I wanted.
My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to make him known.
Come to me all of you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
You will be my slaves and learn from me, for I am gentle and have no pride in me. You will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath day, Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pick some of the grain stalks and eat the heads.
The Pharisees saw this. And they said to Jesus, "Look! This man is breaking the Sabbath law. But your disciples are doing it too!"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read what David did, when he and his men were hungry?
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. The eating was exclusively for the priests.
Have you not read the code? It tells us that the priests in the temple are to perform their duties every Sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
I am telling you that the one who is more important than the temple is here.
The inscription says: "I desire mercy, and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
Proceeding from that place, Jesus entered their synagogue home.
There was a man who had a withered and deformed hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful to heal people on the Sabbath?"
And he said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you seize it and pull it out?"
A man is more valuable than a sheep! So the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath."
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and it was restored, like the other.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus. The chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew everything about the Pharisees' plans. So he left the place. Many followed him, and he healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to tell who it was.
This was to fulfill what had been spoken through the prophet Isaiah, who says:
Here is my servant, whom I have chosen, him who delights me; I am well pleased with him. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will proclaim to the nations that every thing shall be well.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bent branch. He will not extinguish a dim flame. He will fix everything.
Nations shall put their hope in him. (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Beelzebul.
A man possessed by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and unable to speak. Jesus healed him. After that, the man was able to speak and see.
Everyone was amazed. And they said, "Can this be the Son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow is casting out demons by the prince of demons, Belzebub."
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself will be ruined, and every city or household divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is fighting against himself. So how then can his kingdom stand?
You say that you cast out demons by the power of Beelzebub. By whose power, then, do your people cast out demons? So they will become your judges.
But what if the demons are cast out by the power of God? For the Kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you break into a strong man's house and simply take what he owns? You have to tie him up first. Then you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
So here is what I say to you: every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come. This person will not be forgiven, either now or later.
If you make a good tree, its fruit will be good. And if you make a bad tree, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree from its fruit.
You are a nest of poisonous snakes! How can you, the evil ones, say anything good? Out of the abundance of the heart, the mouth speaks.
The good man says good things, and these things come from the goodness preserved within him. And the wicked man says wicked things, and these things come from the wickedness preserved within him.
Here's what I tell you: on the Day of Judgment, people will be questioned about every careless word they uttered.
"In your words, you will be condemned or acquitted." "The Sign of the Miracle of Jonah"
Some Pharisees and law teachers came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered and said, "Evil and adulterous people seek for a miraculous sign! But no sign will be given except the sign of the prophet Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will rise in the resurrection with this generation, and the men of Nineveh will stand forth and condemn this generation, because they repented at the preaching of Jonah; and behold, one greater than Jonah is here.
The Queen of the South will rise on the day of judgment with the people who are now living, and she will prove them guilty. She came from a very long way to witness the wisdom of Solomon. And now here is someone more important than Solomon.
What happens when a demon leaves a person? It wanders through dry areas searching for a place to rest. But it does not find that.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he reaches it, he finds the house empty. It has been completely cleaned and organized.
Then the devil goes away and takes with him seven other spirits more wicked than he is. They enter and live there. That man becomes in a worse state than he was before. This is how it will be with the wicked people of this time." The brothers and sisters of Jesus.
While Jesus was still talking to the crowds, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
Someone told him, "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to speak with you."
Jesus answered and said, "Who is my mother and my brethren?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples and said, "Here are my mother and my brothers!"
"Whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother and sister and mother."
The Farmer's Story
On that same day, Jesus left the house and sat down by the lake of Gennesaret.
Large crowds gathered around him. So he entered a boat. And he sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many stories. And he said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds.
Scattering the seeds on the ground. Some fell on the road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Other seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and intertwined to overwhelm the plants.
Other seeds fell on good ground. They yielded a crop that produced a hundred, sixty, and thirty times more than was sown.
Let him who has ears hear.
The students came to him and asked him, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, saying: "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. It was not given to those outside."
Those who have this type of knowledge will be given more, indeed they will receive a lot. As for those who do not have this type of knowledge, even what little they have will be taken away from them.
Here's why I use stories when I talk to people. I say: "They look, but they don't really see. They listen, but they don't really hear or understand."
In it the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said, "You will hear but not understand, and you will see but not perceive what you are seeing."
Their hearts have become callous, they barely hear, and they have shut their eyes. If they had not done so, they would have seen with their own eyes, and heard with their own ears, and understood with their own hearts. Perhaps they would repent to God and He would heal them. (Isaiah 6: 9, 10)
But blessed are your eyes, for they see; and your ears, for they hear.
What I am going to tell you is true. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the story of the farmer and its meaning.
People hear the message about the kingdom but do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. Those people are like seed sown along the path.
And others took the seed that fell on the rocky ground. These are the ones who hear the word and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. So they last only for a little while. They quickly fall away from the faith when troubles or suffering come because of the message.
And others received the seed that fell among the thorns. They are the ones who hear the message. But then, the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth choke them. So they are not given the chance to produce fruit.
"But others received the seed that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the word and understand it. They will produce fruit, yielding a hundred, sixty or thirty times what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told the crowds another parable. "The Kingdom of Heaven is like this," he said. "A man sowed good seeds in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat started to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's land came to him, saying, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where then have these weeds come from?"
"An enemy did this," he answered. "His servants asked him, 'Do you want us to go and remove the weeds?'"
"No," the owner replied. "While you are weeding, you might pull up the wheat along with it."
Let both grow together until the harvest. At that time I will tell the harvesters to gather the weeds first and bind them in bundles to be burned. Then they are to gather the wheat and bring it into my barn." "The Parables of the Mustard Seed and the Yeast"
Jesus told the crowds another parable, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field.
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest plant in the garden of all. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus said to them, "I will speak to you again in parables: 'The kingdom of heaven is like a leaven which a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, till the whole was leavened.' "
Jesus spoke to all these people using stories. He did not tell them anything without telling a story.
The words of the Prophet opened their way to achieve their purpose. For he said, "I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter dark sayings from of old." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus Explains the Parable of the Weeds
Then Jesus left the crowds and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said to him, "Explain to us the parable of the tares in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the devil.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. And the harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are pulled up and burned in the fire. Thus will it be in the day of judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels, who will remove from his kingdom all causes of sin. They will also remove everyone who does evil.
They will throw them into the burning fever. There people will wail and cry out in pain.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in their father's kingdom. Let him who has ears listen. Stories of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, and out of joy went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
And again, the Kingdom of the heavens is like a merchant seeking a pure pearl.
He found one of great value. So he went and sold all that he had. And he bought that pearl. The story of the net.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the sea, and gathered every kind of fish.
When the net was full, the fishermen drew it to the shore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they threw the bad fish away.
This is how it will be on the Day of Judgment. The angels will come. They will separate those who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
The evil people will be thrown into the glowing furnace. There the evil ones will bark and scream in pain.
Jesus asked: "Do you understand all these things?" They answered: "Yes."
He said to them: "Every scribe who is a trained expert in the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a household who brings out from his storeroom both new and old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories, then moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began to teach people in their synagogue. They were amazed. "Where did this man come from, with this wisdom? Where did he get this power to do miracles?" they asked.
Is not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, among his own people, and in his own house."
He only performed a few miracles there because they did not believe.
John the Baptist's head was cut off.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his companions, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to do miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was saying to Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they considered John to be a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. And Herod was very pleased with her.
He had sworn to her that he would give her anything she asked for.
Herodias told her what to say. And the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he remembered his promise and his dinner guests. So he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod put John the Baptist to death in prison.
His head was brought on a large platter and delivered to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowd heard about it. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed the sick among them.
When evening was at hand, the disciples came to him and said, "This is a deserted place, and it is already late. Send the crowds away, so they can go into the villages and buy food for themselves."
Jesus answered, "They do not need to go away. Give them something to eat."
They answered, "We have only five loaves of bread and two fish."
"Bring them here to me," he said.
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit on the grass. And He took the five loaves and the two fish. And looking up to heaven, He blessed. And He broke the loaves and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. The disciples took up twelve baskets of leftovers.
About 5000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on water.
Immediately, Jesus instructed the disciples to get into the boat. He told them to go ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he sent the crowd away.
After sending them off, he climbed to the top of a mountain to contemplate alone. And when evening came, he was still there alone.
The ship was already far from the shore. The waves were hitting it hard because the winds were blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to His disciples; He walked on the surface of the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were terrified. "He's a ghost!" they cried, and screamed in fear.
Jesus immediately answered, "Don't be afraid! I am. Don't be afraid."
"Master, are you?" Peter asked. "If it is you, tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." And Peter got out of the boat. And he walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out his hand and caught him. "Faithless and perverse generation!" he said, "how much more will I have to bear with you?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
And those in the boat worshiped Jesus, saying, "You are truly the Son of God."
They crossed the lake and reached the region of Gennesaret.
The men who were living there knew Jesus. So they sent a message everywhere in the nearby villages. And people brought every sick person to Jesus.
They asked him to allow the sick to touch only the edge of his garment, and everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked
Why do your disciples not live according to the traditions of the elders? For your disciples do not wash their hands before they eat!
Jesus answered, "Why do you not obey God's command? Instead, you prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother" (Exodus 20:12; Noah's Book 5:16). He also said, "Whoever curses father or mother, let him die the death" (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9).
But you allow people to tell their families: "All the help you have received from us is a gift dedicated to God."
They do not need to honor their parents with this gift. You make the word of God useless in order to follow your own teachings.
"You are the fake ones! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:
These people honor me with what they say. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only man-made laws." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. And He said, "Listen, and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." But what comes out of your mouth, that is what makes you "unclean."
Then his disciples came to him and asked, "Do you not see that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants that my Father in heaven did not plant; they will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Do you not understand yet?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. These are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So do murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also, theft, false testimony, and lying.
"This is the kind of thing that makes you 'unclean.' But eating without washing your hands does not make you 'unclean.'" Faith, a Canaanite woman
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon torments my daughter. She suffers greatly."
Jesus did not utter a word. His disciples came to him and requested him, saying, "Send her away, for she cries out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman knelt before him. And she said, "Lord, help me!"
He answered, saying: "It is not right for us to take the bread of children and throw it to their dogs."
Yes, sir, she said. "But even dogs eat the bread crumbs that fall from their master's table."
Then Jesus answered and said, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus Feeds the Four Thousand
Jesus went away from there, and walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to the top of a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought to him those who were blind and those who could not walk. They also brought those who were lame, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak began to speak. People with disabilities were healed. Those who could not walk began to walk. Those who were blind could see. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to him and said, "I have compassion for these people; they have already been with me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, for they might faint on the way."
His disciples answered and said, "We have no more than five loaves and two fish, unless we are expected to go and buy food for all these people!"
Jesus asked, "How many loaves do you have?" They replied, "Seven loaves, and a few small fish."
Jesus told the multitude to sit down on the ground.
Took the seven loaves of bread and the fish, gave thanks. Then he broke them and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples distributed them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples took seven baskets of leftovers.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into the boat. He went into the region near Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He answered, saying: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say: 'The weather will be fine. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say: "Today will be windy. The sky is red and overcast." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
"A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign. But no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The disciples crossed over to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take bread.
"Beware," Jesus said to them. "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked among themselves about this matter. And they said: "He must be saying this because we did not bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said, "O ye of little faith! Why reason ye among yourselves, saying, 'There is no bread'?"
Don't you understand yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets full of fragments you picked up?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets you filled with the pieces?
How do you not understand at all? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The disciples realized that Jesus was not warning them about the yeast used in bread, but was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went to the region of Caesarea Philippi, and there he asked his disciples, "What do people say about the Son of Man?"
They answered: "Some say John the Baptist, others Elijah, and still others that they seem to see Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered and said, "Blessed are you, Simon, son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but My Father who is in heaven.
Here is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
"I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven."
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
Since that time, Jesus began explaining to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem, and that there they would suffer much affliction from the chief priests, elders, and religious leaders. He must be killed, and on the third day rise from the dead.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him, saying, "No, Lord! This will never happen to you!"
When Jesus turned around and said to Peter, "Get out of my way, Satan! You are thinking not as God does, but as people do."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "If anyone wants to follow me, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he sacrifices his life for me, he will find a better life.
What good would it do a person to gain the whole world but lose their own soul? Or what could a person possibly give in exchange for their soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will repay each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Some of those standing here will not die before they see the coming of the Son of Man in his kingdom.
Jesus' appearance was changed.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John his brother, and brought them up to a high mountain by themselves.
Before them, his appearance changed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as daylight.
At that very moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still talking, a bright cloud came over them. And a voice out of the cloud said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were afraid and fell on their faces.
But Jesus came and touched them. And He said, "Arise, and do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They went down the mountain. And as they were coming down, Jesus told them what they must do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. It is necessary for Elijah to come and restore all things.
But I tell you, Elijah has come, and they have not recognized him, but have done to him whatever they pleased." So also will the Son of Man suffer at their hands."
So the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they came near the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus; he knelt down before him.
"Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He is shaking violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples, but they were not able to heal him.
"O ye people, O ye infidels, O ye wicked!" Jesus answered. "How long shall I still be with you? And how long shall I still bear you? Bring the boy to me."
Jesus commanded the demon to leave the boy, and it departed from him. He was immediately healed.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and asked, "Why could we not drive out the demon?"
He answered, "Because your faith is very weak. What I will tell you is the truth. If you have faith as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Be removed from here to there,' and it will be removed. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They met in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." When the disciples heard this, they were filled with sorrow. Jesus Pays the Temple Tax
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, a tax collector came to Peter and asked him, "Does not your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does," he answered. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "From whom do the kings of the earth take toll and tribute? From their own sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then children will not be forced to pay," Jesus told him.
But we do not want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line. And take the very first fish that you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin that you need. Take it and give it to them for each of our taxes.
